 Green Growth\nL1813@P54: Studies, OECD Publishing, Paris, http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264190504-en.\nL1814@P54: [1]\nL1815@P54: OECD (2012), OECD Environmental Outlook to 2050: The Consequences of Inaction, OECD\nL1816@P54: Publishing, Paris, http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264122246-en.\nL1817@P54: [5]\nL1818@P54: OECD (2008), Measuring material flows and resource productivity; Volume I, The OECD\nL1819@P54: Guide, OECD, PARIS, https://www.oecd.org/environment/indicators-modelling\u0002outlooks/MFA-Guide.pdf (accessed on 06 February 2018).\nL1820@P54: [7]\nL1821@P54: Schandl, H. (2017), “Global Material Flows and Resource Productivity: Forty Years of\nL1822@P54: Evidence”, Journal of Industrial Ecology , Vol. 46/1, http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/jiec.12626.\nL1823@P54: [21]\nL1824@P54: Stadler, K. et al. (2018), “EXIOBASE 3: Developing a Time Series of Detailed Environmentally\nL1825@P54: Extended Multi-Regional Input-Output Tables”, Journal of Industrial Ecology,\nL1826@P54: http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/jiec.12715.\nL1827@P54-55: [16]54 │ CHAPTER 2. MODELLING FUTURE MATERIALS USE AND ITS ECONOMIC DRIVERS\nL1828@P55: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL1829@P55: UN (2017), “World Population Prospects: key findings and advance tables”,\nL1830@P55: https://esa.un.org/unpd/wpp/publications/Files/WPP2017_KeyFindings.pdf (accessed on\nL1831@P55: 18 May 2018).\nL1832@P55: [4]\nL1833@P55: UNEP (2017), Resource Efficiency: Potential and Economic Implications. A report of the\nL1834@P55: International Resource Panel., Ekins, P., Hughes, N., et al..\nL1835@P55: [12]\nL1836@P55: USGS (2017), Critical mineral resources of the United States—Economic and environmental\nL1837@P55: geology and prospects for future supply, http://dx.doi.org/10.3133/pp1802.\nL1838@P55: [23]\nL1839@P55: Wiedmann, T. et al. (2015), “The material footprint of nations.”, Proceedings of the National\nL1840@P55: Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, Vol. 112/20, pp. 6271-6,\nL1841@P55: http://dx.doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1220362110.\nL1842@P55: [19]\nL1843@P55: Winning, M. et al. (2017), “Towards a circular economy: insights based on the development of\nL1844@P55: the global ENGAGE-materials model and evidence for the iron and steel industry”,\nL1845@P55: International Economics and Economic Policy, Vol. 14/3, pp. 383-407,\nL1846@P55: http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s10368-017-0385-3.\nL1847@P55-56: [8]CHAPTER 2. MODELLING FUTURE MATERIALS USE AND ITS ECONOMIC DRIVERS │ 55\nL1848@P56: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL1849@P56: Annex 2.A. The modelling framework\nL1850@P56: 2.A.1 The modelling approach to produce economic and materials projections\nL1851@P56: The process to produce economic and materials projection relies on a suite of modelling\nL1852@P56: tools, as described in Section 2.2. As a first step, the OECD ENV-Growth model, a\nL1853@P56: macroeconomic growth model based on a conditional convergence framework, projects\nL1854@P56: global macroeconomic trends by country. It does so by using a set of assumptions on\nL1855@P56: long-term macro trends such as demography or technical progress, in addition to short\u0002term forecasts and long-term projections of the OECD Economics Department and the\nL1856@P56: International Monetary Fund (IMF).\nL1857@P56: The output of ENV-Growth is an essential input for the OECD’s multisectoral ENV\u0002Linkages model, which focuses on the evolution of sectoral and regional economic\nL1858@P56: activity and the associated economy-environment interactions in the coming decades. In\nL1859@P56: fact, as a second step, the projections of GDP, investment and trade balances from ENV\u0002Growth, are imposed on ENV-Linkages in the calibration of the baseline scenarios. These\nL1860@P56: GDP projections are then endogenised in ENV-Linkages to allow assessment of the\nL1861@P56: impacts of alternative scenarios. Thus, the exogenous parameters in ENV-Linkages, e.g.\nL1862@P56: on productivity, are adjusted in the calibration process to mimic the macroeconomic\nL1863@P56: growth paths from ENV-Growth. ENV-Linkages and ENV-Growth are thus mutually\nL1864@P56: consistent. As a last step, as ENV-Linkages is enhanced to include data on materials use\nL1865@P56: and recycling. In ENV-Linkages material flow projections follow the evolution of the\nL1866@P56: production and consumption of goods in different sectors and regions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlobal Material Resources Outlook to 2060 (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2019/02/global-material-resources-outlook-to-2060_g1g98d7d/9789264307452-en.pdf)\nciteturn247view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 214; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn245view1\",\"lineno\":5695}); Total lines: 6041\nL5662@P201: horizon dependency of toxicity potentials calculated with the multi-media fate, exposure and\nL5663@P201: effects model USES-LCA., Institute for Biodiversity and Ecosystem Dynamics, University of\nL5664@P201: Amsterdam, http://www.leidenuniv.nl/interfac/cml/lca2/.\nL5665@P201: [24]\nL5668@P201: Environmental Science, Faculty of Environmental Science, University of Amsterdam, The\nL5669@P201: Netherlands, https://media.leidenuniv.nl/legacy/Life-cycle%20impact%20assessment.pdf\nL5670@P201: (accessed on 20 September 2018).\nL5671@P201: [19]\nL5672@P201: Huijbregts, M. (1999), Priority assessment of toxic substances in LCA. Development and\nL5673@P201: application of the multi-media fate, exposure and effect model USES-LCA, IVAM\nL5674@P201: environmental research, University of Amsterdam.\nL5675@P201: [25]\nL5676@P201: IEA (2017), World Energy Outlook 2017, OECD Publishing, Paris/IEA, Paris,\nL5677@P201: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/weo-2017-en.\nL5678@P201-202: [10]CHAPTER 8. PROJECTIONS OF THE ENVIRONMENTAL CONSEQUENCES OF MATERIALS USE │ 201\nL5679@P202: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES 