ries production (metric tons) | Data,\nL5740@P203: https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/ER.FSH.PROD.MT (accessed on 28 March 2018).\nL5741@P203: [4]\nL5742@P203: WWF (2018), Pulp and paper | WWF,\nL5743@P203: http://wwf.panda.org/about_our_earth/deforestation/forest_sector_transformation/pulp_and_p\nL5744@P203: aper/ (accessed on 28 March 2018).\nL5745@P203-204: [3]CHAPTER 8. PROJECTIONS OF THE ENVIRONMENTAL CONSEQUENCES OF MATERIALS USE │ 203\nL5746@P204: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL5747@P204: Annex 8.A. Detailed results and supplementary materials\nL5748@P204: 8.A.1. Comparing the GHG projections to the literature\nL5749@P204: The global GHG emission projections can be compared to earlier projections, especially\nL5750@P204: the range of baseline projections used for the Fifth Assessment Report of the IPCC\nL5751@P204: (IPCC, 2014[13]), the projections of the Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (Riahi et al.,\nL5752@P204: 2015[14]) and earlier OECD projections (see Figure 8.A.1).\nL5753@P204: Figure 8.A.1. Global GHG emissions projections compared to the literature\nL5754@P204: Gt CO2-equivalent, including LULUCF\nL5755@P204: Note: Emissions from land use, land use change and forestry, as projected in (OECD, 2012[46]), added to the\nL5756@P204: ENV-Linkages scenario to ensure comparability.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlobal Material Resources Outlook to 2060 (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2019/02/global-material-resources-outlook-to-2060_g1g98d7d/9789264307452-en.pdf)\nciteturn247view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 214; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn245view1\",\"lineno\":3467}); Total lines: 6041\nL3441@P111: expense of the labour share.\nL3442@P111: 13 The number for this scenario do not match those of (IEA, 2017[25]), as the macroeconomic\nL3443@P111: projections are harmonised with the central baseline projection.\nL3444@P111: 14 As discussed in (IEA, 2017[25]), the Current Policies Scenario “[…] assumes slower progress in\nL3445@P111: the transition towards a services-oriented economic growth model than in the New Policies\nL3446@P111: Scenario: the share of services in total GDP rises to 60% only by around 2040, which is ten years\nL3447@P111: later than in the New Policies Scenario. Overall, the Current Policies Scenario achieves the same\nL3448@P111: level of economic growth as the New Policies Scenario over the projection period […]. But the\nL3449@P111: drivers of growth are different between the scenarios, and this has major implications for the\nL3450@P111: structure and shape of the economy in 2040, and for its energy sector.”\nL3451@P111: References\nL3452@P111: Bleischwitz, R. et al. (2018), “Extrapolation or saturation – Revisiting growth patterns,\nL3453@P111: development stages and decoupling”, Global Environmental Change, Vol. 48, pp. 86-96,\nL3454@P111: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/J.GLOENVCHA.2017.11.008.\nL3455@P111: [1]\nL3456@P111: Fishman, T. et al. (2014), “Accounting for the Material Stock of Nations”, Journal of Industrial\nL3457@P111: Ecology, http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/jiec.12114.\nL3458@P111: [2]\nL3459@P111: IEA (2017), World Energy Outlook 2017, OECD Publishing, Paris/IEA, Paris,\nL3460@P111: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/weo-2017-en.\nL3461@P111: [5]\nL3462@P111: Long, J. and L. Summers (1991), “Equipment Investment and Economic Growth”, The Quarterly\nL3463@P111: Journal of Economics, Vol. 106/2, p. 445, http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/2937944.\nL3464@P111: [6]\nL3465@P111: Müller, D., T. Wang and B. Duval (2011), “Patterns of iron use in societal evolution”,\nL3466@P111: Environmental Science and Technology, http://dx.doi.org/10.1021/es102273t.\nL3467@P111-112: [3]CHAPTER 4. PROJECTIONS OF THE ECONOMIC DRIVERS OF MATERIALS USE │ 111\nL3468@P112: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL3469@P112: Vehmas, J., J. Luukkanen and J. Kaivo-oja (2007), “Linking analyses and environmental\nL3470@P112: Kuznets curves for aggregated material flows in the EU”, Journal of Cleaner Production,\nL3471@P112: Vol. 15/17, pp. 1662-1673, http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/J.JCLEPRO.2006.08.010.\nL3472@P112-113: [4]112 │ CHAPTER 4. PROJECTIONS OF THE ECONOMIC DRIVERS OF MATERIALS USE\nL3473@P113: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL3474@P113: \nL3475@P113: Annex 4.A. Detailed results and supplementary materials\nL3476@P113: Figure 4.A.1. The composition of investment expenditure by commodity\nL3477@P113-114: Source: OECD ENV-Linkages model. CHAPTER 4. PROJECTIONS OF THE ECONOMIC DRIVERS OF MATERIALS USE │ 113\nL3478@P114: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL3479@P114: Figure 4.A.2. The production of the construction sector by region\nL3480@P114: Evolution of the production of the construction sector in USD (2011 PPP), index 1 in 2017.\nL3481@P114-115: Source: OECD ENV-Linkages model. 114 │ CHAPTER 4. PROJECTIONS OF THE ECONOMIC DRIVERS OF MATERIALS USE\nL3482@P115: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL3483@P115: Figure 4.A.3. Construction materials intensity of GDP by region\nL3484@P115: Construction materials intensity of GDP by region measured as the ratio of materials use to real GDP.\nL3485@P115-116: Source: OECD ENV-Linkages model. PART II. MATERIALS USE TO 2060 │ 115\nL3486@P116: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL3487@P116: Part II.\nL3488@P116-118: Materials use to 2060CHAPTER 5. PROJECTIONS OF PRIMARY MATERIALS USE │ 117\nL3489@P118: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL3490@P118: Chapter 5.\nL3491@P118: Projections of primary materials use\nL3492@P118: This chapter presents projections of materials use to 2060 at the global and regional\nL3493@P118: level for four types of materials: biomass, fossil fuels, metals, and non-metallic minerals.\nL3494@P118: These projections focus on three main indicators: primary materials use, materials\nL3495@P118: intensity of GDP and per-capita materials use. The chapter analyses the future dynamics\nL3496@P118: of materials use for these three different aspects, as well as their links to alternative\nL3497@P118: economic and population growth scenarios.\nL3498@P118: This document, as well as any data and any map included herein, are without prejudice to\nL3499@P118: the status of or sovereignty over any territory, to the delimitation of international frontiers\nL3500@P118-119: and boundaries and to the name of any territory, city or area. 118 │ CHAPTER 5. PROJECTIONS OF PRIMARY MATERIALS USE\nL3501@P119: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL3502@P119: \nL3503@P119: KEY MESSAGES\nL3504@P119: To understand the environmental consequences of materials use, and the impacts of\nL3505@P119: policies designed to promote resource efficiency and stimulate the transition to a\nL3506@P119: circular economy, the first step is to take stock of the current use of primary materials\nL3507@P119: (virgin materials sourced from mining and extraction activities). For all countries, non\u0002metallic minerals constitute the bulk of materials used. The use of materials however\nL3508@P119: varies by country, and particularly depends on their development level. Notably, China\nL3509@P119: is a large user of materials given the size of its economy and its need for construction\nL3510@P119: materials to build infrastructure.\nL3511@P119: Projections and trends\nL3512@P119:  The use of primary materials is projected to almost double over the coming 5\nL3513@P119: decades in the central baseline scenario, rising from 89 gigatonnes (Gt) in 2017\nL3514@P119: to 167 Gt in 2060. In the absence of new policies to improve resource\nL3515@P119: efficiency and stimulate the transition to a circular economy, materials use is\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlobal Material Resources Outlook to 2060 (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2019/02/global-material-resources-outlook-to-2060_g1g98d7d/9789264307452-en.pdf)\nciteturn247view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 214; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn245view1\",\"lineno\":4460}); Total lines: 6041\nL4433@P157: Figure 6.11. The share of secondary metals is projected to change little under alternative\nL4434@P157: population and income convergence assumptions.\nL4435@P157: Evolution of the share of secondary metal in percentage of total production\nL4436@P157: Source: OECD ENV-Linkages model.\nL4437@P157: StatLink 2 https://doi.org/10.1787/888933885391\nL4438@P157: Notes\nL4439@P157: 1\nL4440@P157:  In some cases, recycled content is higher. This can be the case when a lot of material is recovered\nL4441@P157: at the production stage: these materials will then not be counted towards end-of-life recycling as\nL4442@P157: they do not go through the waste handling process.\nL4443@P157: 2\nL4444@P157:  This includes e.g. recycling boxes for transporting the produce.\nL4445@P157: 3\nL4446@P157:  These figures only include extraction of primary materials and provision of secondary materials,\nL4447@P157: not their (re-)processing which carried out by the corresponding industrial sectors.\nL4448@P157: References\nL4449@P157: ABREE (2016), Resources and Energy Statistics, https://industry.gov.au/Office-of-the-Chief\u0002Economist/Publications/Pages/Resources-and-energy-statistics.aspx (accessed on\nL4450@P157: 18 May 2018).\nL4451@P157: [3]\nL4452@P157: Stadler, K. et al. (2018), “EXIOBASE 3: Developing a Time Series of Detailed Environmentally\nL4453@P157: Extended Multi-Regional Input-Output Tables”, Journal of Industrial Ecology,\nL4454@P157: http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/jiec.12715.\nL4455@P157-158: [8]CHAPTER 6. PROJECTIONS OF RECYCLING AND SECONDARY MATERIALS │ 157\nL4456@P158: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL4457@P158: UNEP (2016), Global Waste Management Outlook, UN, New York,\nL4458@P158: http://dx.doi.org/10.18356/765baec0-en.\nL4459@P158: [5]\nL4460@P158: UNEP (2013), Metal Recycling – Opportunitites, Limits, Infrastructure (Full report), United\nL4461@P158: Nations Environment Programme,\nL4462@P158: http://www.resourcepanel.org/sites/default/files/documents/document/media/e\u0002book_metals_report2b_recyclingopportunities_130919.pdf (accessed on 18 May 2018).\nL4463@P158: [6]\nL4464@P158: UNEP (2011), Recycling Rates of Metals: a status report, International Resource Panel,\nL4465@P158: http://wedocs.unep.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.11822/8702/-\nL4466@P158: Recycling%20rates%20of%20metals%3a%20A%20status%20report\u00022011Recycling_Rates.pdf?sequence=3&isAllowed=y (accessed on 16 May 2018).\nL4467@P158: [1]\nL4468@P158: USGS (2016), USGS Minerals Information: Copper,\nL4469@P158: https://minerals.usgs.gov/minerals/pubs/commodity/copper/index.html#myb (accessed on\nL4470@P158: 18 May 2018).\nL4471@P158: [4]\nL4472@P158: World Bank (2018), What a Waste 2.0: A Global Snapshot of Solid Waste Management to 2050,\nL4473@P158: World Bank, Washington, DC.\nL4474@P158: [7]\nL4475@P158-159: Worldsteel (2018), Worldsteel, https://www.worldsteel.org/ (accessed on 18 May 2018). [2]158 │ CHAPTER 6. PROJECTIONS OF RECYCLING AND SECONDARY MATERIALS\nL4476@P159: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL4477@P159: \nL4478@P159: Annex 6.A. Detailed results and supplementary materials\nL4479@P159: The recycling sector was split from the manufacturing sector in the GTAP database using\nL4480@P159: the structure from the Exiobase database (see Figure 6.A.1). Panel A shows the\nL4481@P159: production structure of the recycling service: most of the cost structure of the sector\nL4482@P159: consists of labour costs (27 %), and capital costs (21 %). Furthermore, this sector overall\nL4483@P159: benefits from no taxes applied to it. Furthermore, 18 % of production costs are dedicated\nL4484@P159: to Services while 8 % are dedicated to self-consumption in the recycling sector itself.\nL4485@P159: Finally, land transport is a key input as goods need to be collected.\nL4486@P159: Interestingly, the tax rates are very heterogeneous across countries for the recycling sector\nL4487@P159: (see Figure 2.A.1). While input tax rates are positive for all countries – ranging from -\nL4488@P159: 0.1 % to 10.2 % – other production tax rates are very small. A remarkable figure is China\nL4489@P159: with a very high subsidy, leading to a -13.6 % tax rate. Other countries range from -0.9 %\nL4490@P159: to 1.5 %. The high subsidy in China explains the global tax rate close to zero (in\nL4491@P159: Figure 6.A.1).\nL4492@P159: Figure 6.A.1, Panel B depicts the demand structure. While 10 % goes to Investment, and\nL4493@P159: 4 % to households, the largest consumer is the Iron and steel processing (primary for\nL4494@P159: 10 % as well as secondary for 8 %). Self-consumption also constitutes the end point of\nL4495@P159: 8 % of the sector production. Aside from steel, the ‘Vegetable and fruits’ sector is one of\nL4496@P159: the big consumer of this service with 5 % (for the pallet boxes?). The governmental\nL4497@P159: services uses about 4 %, while plastics, paper and textiles appear next (respectively 4 %,\nL4498@P159: 4 %, and 3 %). Construction only uses about 2 %, while at the same levels as ‘Fabricated\nL4499@P159-160: metal products’, ‘Aluminium Primary’ and ‘Aluminium Secondary’.CHAPTER 6. PROJECTIONS OF RECYCLING AND SECONDARY MATERIALS │ 159\nL4500@P160: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL4501@P160: Figure 6.A.1. Global production and demand structure of the recycling sector\nL4502@P160-161: Source: Own compilation from Exiobase 3 database (Stadler et al., 2018[8]). 160 │ CHAPTER 6. PROJECTIONS OF RECYCLING AND SECONDARY MATERIALS\nL4503@P161: GLOBAL MATERIAL RESOURCES OUTLOOK TO 2060 © OECD 2019\nL4504@P161: \nL4505@P161: Figure 6.A.2. Production tax rates for recycling\nL4506@P161-162: Source: Own compilation from Exiobase 3 database (Stadler et al., 2018[8]). CHAPTER 7. CASE STUDIES ON DEMAND AND SUPPLY RISKS FOR SPECIFIC MATERIALS │ 161\n"
